{# Everything inside <body>: the app bar, the layout, the toasts and the topics dialog.
   Rendered on page load by shell.html.j2, and patched WHOLE on every interaction ("fat morph").

   The Tao of Datastar: "Morphing ensures that only modified parts of the DOM are updated, preserving
   state and improving performance. This allows you to send down large chunks of the DOM tree (all the
   way up to the `html` tag), sometimes known as 'fat morph'". So this is the unit of update, and the
   server stops having to remember which fragments a change touches -- the class of bug that let a
   clamped `difficulty` sit stale in the sidebar.

   Two things stay OUTSIDE this region, on <body>, precisely because they must not be re-created by a
   morph: the signal declarations, and `data-init` (which in stream mode opens the timer connection --
   re-running it per patch would leak a held connection each time). #}
    {# An app bar with a nav toggle, which is the useful half of what panel's MaterialTemplate
       provided. Collapsing the sidebar is view state, so it is a local signal: instant, and the
       server is never told. #}
    <header class="topbar">
      <button type="button" class="nav-toggle" aria-label="Toggle sidebar"
              data-attr:aria-expanded="$_navOpen"
              data-on:click="$_navOpen = !$_navOpen">
        <span class="bars" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </button>
      <h1>{{ variant.title }}</h1>
      {# The theme toggle, on the CHROME side of the bar -- left of the spacer, with the hamburger.

         Not in the drawer, because the drawer starts closed and "this is too bright right now" is
         fixed the moment it is noticed. Not in the HUD either, which is the mistake this was moved
         out of: the bar is two clusters split by `.topbar-spacer`, chrome on the left and game state
         on the right, and a palette is not a move in the game. It also should not neighbour Skip --
         that is the one control that SPENDS something, and a mis-tap there costs a skip.

         Three states in one button: auto (the OS decides, the default) -> light -> dark. Auto is a
         real state, not a synonym for light: it is the only one that follows the machine when it
         switches at sunset, and it is what `data-theme` being ABSENT means. Purely client-side --
         `$_theme` is local and the CSS does the rest through `light-dark()`, so unlike Skip this
         works while an answer is in flight and is never disabled.
         The glyphs are symbol-font TEXT, not emoji, so they take the app bar's white the way the
         hamburger's bars do. #}
      <button type="button" class="theme-toggle button"
              data-attr:aria-label="'Colour theme: ' + $_theme + '. Activate to change.'"
              data-attr:title="'Theme: ' + $_theme"
              {# The browser writes the cookie; the server reads it on the next load and renders
                 `data-theme` into the first paint (see `render.THEME_COOKIE`). A year, so it is a
                 preference rather than a session quirk, and path-scoped exactly like the session
                 cookie so two apps under one host do not overwrite each other. `lax` because this
                 is only ever read by our own top-level navigations. #}
              data-on:click="$_theme = $_theme === 'auto' ? 'light' : ($_theme === 'light' ? 'dark' : 'auto');
                document.cookie = '{{ theme_cookie }}=' + $_theme + ';path={{ cookie_path }};max-age=31536000;samesite=lax'">
        <span aria-hidden="true"
              data-text="$_theme === 'light' ? '☀' : ($_theme === 'dark' ? '☾' : '◐')">◐</span>
      </button>
      <span class="topbar-spacer"></span>

      {# THE HUD: live state and the one action you take *during* a question.

         It used to be split badly. The app bar showed the score, the sidebar showed the same numbers
         again in a `#score` panel, and `Skip` -- which spends a resource mid-question -- was filed in
         the sidebar next to the controls that RESTART the quiz. So using a skip meant opening a
         settings drawer, and the score existed twice.

         Sorted by when you touch it: live state and Skip live here, always visible; anything that
         restarts the quiz lives in the drawer, which now starts closed. #}
      {# The streak GROWS with the run (`transform: scale`, transitioned in CSS, so each correct
         answer is a visible step rather than a redraw) and warms through two colour bands.
         `Math.min` caps the growth at 8 -- an unbounded scale eventually reflows the app bar. Hidden
         entirely at streak 0 and when the game-feel toggle is off, both in `juice.css`: a chip
         reading "0×" is worse than no chip.

         It SAYS "streak", because a bare "3×" is a rebus: the panel it used to be explained by
         (`Progress`) is collapsed by default, so the app bar has to stand on its own. The word is
         dropped again under 560px, where the bar has no room for it -- the `aria-label` carries it
         at every width, so the meaning never depends on the space. #}
      <span class="streak"
            data-attr:aria-label="'streak ' + $_streak"
            data-class="{hot: $_streak >= 3, blazing: $_streak >= 6, cold: $_streak < 1}"
            data-style:transform="'scale(' + (1 + Math.min($_streak, 8) * 0.06) + ')'">
        <span class="streak-label" aria-hidden="true">streak</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true" data-text="$_streak"></span><span aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
      </span>

      {# The fraction is wrapped so the CSS can shed it on a narrow bar. It is the rung after the
         gauge (dropped at 760px) on the same ladder: under 400px the bar keeps the POINTS, because
         points are what the skips are earned against, and the fraction is still on the drawer's
         dial. Wrapping it here rather than hiding `.topbar-score` whole is the difference between
         a smaller score and no score. #}
      <span class="topbar-score">
        <span class="score-fraction"><span data-text="$_correct"></span>/<span data-text="$_attempted"></span>&nbsp;·&nbsp;</span><span data-text="$_points"></span> pts
      </span>

      {# The points gauge, promoted out of the sidebar. The NOTCHES are why it earns space up here:
         each one is a skip you can earn, so the bar answers "how close am I to another skip" at a
         glance. The gradient stays on the track with a mask over the unearned part, so a colour means
         the same number of points wherever the bar has reached (it was painted on the growing fill
         once, which squeezed the whole red-to-green ramp into whatever had been earned). #}
      <div class="meter points-meter hud-meter" role="img"
           data-class="{full: $_pointsPct >= 100}"
           data-attr:aria-label="'points ' + $_points + ' of {{ points_goal }}'">
        <div class="meter-mask" data-style:width="(100 - $_pointsPct) + '%'"></div>
        {% for milestone in milestones if milestone < 1 %}
        {% set pct = (milestone * 100) | round | int %}
        <span class="meter-tick" style="left: {{ pct }}%"
              data-class="{earned: $_pointsPct >= {{ pct }}}"></span>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>

      {# Skip: an in-play action, so it is in the HUD rather than the drawer. `s` works too -- same
         guards as the digit accelerators (not while an answer is in flight, not while a form control
         has focus), plus the button's own disabled conditions restated, because a window handler does
         not inherit them. #}
      <button type="button" class="skip warning button is-warning"
              data-attr:disabled="$_skipsLeft <= 0 || !$_playing"
              data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/skip{{ variant_query }}')"
              data-on:keydown__window="evt.key === 's'
                && $_skipsLeft > 0 && $_playing && !$_answering && !$_topicsOpen
                && !evt.target.closest?.('{{ TYPING_TARGETS }}')
                && @post('{{ prefix }}/skip{{ variant_query }}')">
        Skip <span class="skip-count" data-text="$_skipsLeft"></span>
      </button>
    </header>

    <div class="layout" data-class="{'nav-closed': !$_navOpen}">
      {# Dismiss-on-outside-click, but NOT when the click was the toggle itself: the hamburger sits
         outside the drawer, so opening it also counted as an outside click and the two handlers
         cancelled each other out. Same shape as the reveal's double-bound Enter -- one gesture must
         not drive two handlers. #}
      <aside class="sidebar"
             data-on:click__outside="$_navOpen && window.matchMedia('{{ DRAWER_OVERLAY_QUERY }}').matches && !evt.target.closest('.nav-toggle') && ($_navOpen = false)">
        {# What is left of the old `#score` panel: the dial, in a collapsed group.

           The numbers and the points gauge moved to the HUD (they were duplicated in the app bar
           anyway). The dial stays because it is pleasant and panel had one, but it spends a lot of
           space on a single number, so it is folded away rather than occupying the top of the column.
           `Skip` moved to the HUD too -- it is an in-play action, and it did not belong beside the
           controls that restart the quiz. #}
        {# `data-preserve-attr="open"`: `open` is state the PLAYER set, and the server has no idea about
           it -- so a fat morph, which rewrites `#app` from markup that never carries the attribute, snapped
           every disclosure shut on every answer, skip, settings change and restart. Only System Notes had
           this, so only System Notes survived. See DESIGN.md. #}
        <details class="panel box progress" id="progress" data-preserve-attr="open">
          <summary>Progress</summary>
          {# The panel Dial, by hand. One 90-radius arc, coloured by band and revealed with
             stroke-dashoffset -- so the value is a signal expression and no markup is patched.
             `PI * 90 = 282.74` is the full sweep. #}
          <svg class="dial" viewBox="0 0 200 110" role="img" aria-label="percentage correct">
            <path class="dial-track" d="M 10 100 A 90 90 0 0 1 190 100" />
            <path class="dial-value" d="M 10 100 A 90 90 0 0 1 190 100"
                  data-style:stroke-dashoffset="282.74 * (1 - $_scorePct / 100)"
                  data-class="{poor: $_scorePct < 30, weak: $_scorePct >= 30 && $_scorePct < 49, fair: $_scorePct >= 49 && $_scorePct < 59, ok: $_scorePct >= 59 && $_scorePct < 75, good: $_scorePct >= 75}" />
            <text class="dial-text" x="100" y="95" data-text="$_scorePct + '%'">0%</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="score-line">
            <span data-text="$_correct"></span> of <span data-text="$_attempted"></span> right
            &middot; <span data-text="$_points"></span> / {{ points_goal }} points
          </p>
        </details>

        {# The debug panel: the panel app's `debug_enabled` row of buttons, for reaching states that
           take minutes of honest play. Rendered only when the session is armed -- `?debug` on a page
           load, or `DSQUIZ_DEBUG=1` (what `just dev` sets). See `app.DEBUG_MODE`.

           Not styled beyond a warning tint on purpose: it should look like scaffolding, so nobody
           mistakes it for part of the quiz or leaves it switched on. #}
        {% if debug %}
        <section class="panel box debug" id="debug">
          <strong>Debug</strong>
          <div class="debug-row">
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/debug/points/100{{ variant_query }}')">+100 pts</button>
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/debug/points/-100{{ variant_query }}')">&minus;100</button>
          </div>
          <div class="debug-row">
            {# the goal decides completion AND where the skip milestones fall, so a small goal
               exercises the whole ladder in a minute #}
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/debug/goal/200{{ variant_query }}')">goal 200</button>
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/debug/goal/1000{{ variant_query }}')">goal 1000</button>
          </div>
          <div class="debug-row">
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/debug/reveal{{ variant_query }}')">show reveal</button>
            <button type="button" class="button is-small" data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/debug/complete{{ variant_query }}')">show finale</button>
          </div>
          {# `build` is the fingerprint of the server-side sources (`render.build_stamp`). It is here
             because "the app is broken again" has three times been a process serving code from
             before the fix: templates are re-read per render, this module is not, so a half-reloaded
             server renders new markup with stale constants. If the stamp does not move after an
             edit, the server did not reload -- restart it before debugging anything else. #}
          <p class="hint">
            goal {{ points_goal }} &middot; qid {{ qid }} &middot;
            {{ 'playing' if playing else 'finished' }} &middot; build {{ build_stamp }}
          </p>
        </section>
        {% endif %}

        <section class="panel box controls">
          <label>
            Difficulty <output data-text="$difficulty"></output>
            {# `change`, not `input`: fires on release, like panel's value_throttled -- but
               debounced, because with the KEYBOARD `change` fires on every arrow keypress and a
               settings change restarts the quiz, so holding an arrow key wiped the score.
               `value` is server-rendered too: a range input with no value defaults to the
               midpoint of its span, which is what the browser paints before datastar's
               binding has run. #}
            <input type="range" min="{{ min_difficulty }}" max="{{ max_difficulty }}" step="1"
                   value="{{ settings.difficulty }}"
                   data-bind:difficulty data-on:change__debounce.400ms="@post('{{ prefix }}/settings{{ variant_query }}')" />
          </label>
          {# `checked` is server-rendered for the same reason as the slider `value`s, and it
             matters more here: an unchecked box on first paint is state the binding would
             push back up, quietly turning ladder mode off. #}
          <label class="checkbox">
            <input type="checkbox" {% if settings.ladder_mode %}checked{% endif %}
                   data-bind:ladder-mode data-on:change="@post('{{ prefix }}/settings{{ variant_query }}')" />
            Ladder mode (can lose points)
          </label>
          <label class="checkbox">
            <input type="checkbox" {% if settings.target_on %}checked{% endif %}
                   data-bind:target-on data-on:change="@post('{{ prefix }}/settings{{ variant_query }}')" />
            Target percentage required
          </label>
          <label>
            Target <output data-text="$targetPct"></output>%
            <input type="range" min="70" max="90" step="10" value="{{ settings.target_pct }}"
                   data-attr:disabled="!$targetOn"
                   data-bind:target-pct data-on:change__debounce.400ms="@post('{{ prefix }}/settings{{ variant_query }}')" />
          </label>
          {# Topics before the filter, and the filter folded away behind "Advanced", because they
             answer the same question at two very different prices: a topic is a name you recognise
             ("Weak twos"), the filter is a pattern language with six rules. Anyone can use the first;
             the second is for someone who already knows what `1D-1M-1N` means. The filter box was on
             top for months purely because it was built first.
             The STATUS line stays outside the fold: it says what the working set currently is, which
             is worth seeing whether or not you are editing a pattern. #}
          {% if topics %}
          {# Opening the picker closes the drawer on a narrow screen: the button is IN the drawer, so on a
             phone you would otherwise be looking at a dialog stacked on top of the thing you opened it
             from. On a wide screen the drawer is a column beside the dialog, so it stays. #}
          <button type="button" class="light button is-light"
                  data-on:click="$_topicsOpen = true; window.matchMedia('{{ DRAWER_OVERLAY_QUERY }}').matches && ($_navOpen = false)">Topics&hellip;</button>
          {% endif %}
          <div class="filter-status" id="filter-status">{{ filter_status | safe }}</div>
          <details class="advanced" data-preserve-attr="open">
            <summary>Advanced: bidding tree filter</summary>
            {# The bidding-tree filter. `$filterText` is the *draft* -- typed but not applied --
               which is client state by definition (panel modelled the same split as
               `value_input` vs `value`). Every keystroke asks the server what it would select;
               only Enter commits. The validation stays where `bidfilter` is, so the browser
               knows nothing about bidding. #}
            <label>
              Bidding tree filter
              <input type="text" class="input" list="topic-names" placeholder="e.g. 1D-1M-1N, or a topic"
                     value="{{ filter_text }}"
                     data-bind:filter-text
                     data-on:input__debounce.300ms="@get('{{ prefix }}/filter/preview{{ variant_query }}')"
                     {# Enter COMMITS, and a commit is the end of the interaction -- so it hands focus
                        back, exactly as the Appearance pickers do. Typing is not: `input` above must
                        never blur, or the box would eject you after one character.
                        Why it matters beyond tidiness: this input keeps focus after Enter, and a
                        focused text box legitimately swallows every digit -- so applying a filter and
                        then reaching for 1-9 looked like "the accelerators are broken again", with the
                        keystrokes quietly landing in the filter box. The blur is FIRST in the sequence
                        so the post is not racing a focus change. #}
                     data-on:keydown="evt.key === 'Enter' && (evt.target.blur(), @post('{{ prefix }}/filter/apply{{ variant_query }}'))" />
            </label>
            {# native autocomplete: the topic names, no javascript of ours #}
            <datalist id="topic-names">
              {% for topic in topics %}
              <option value="{{ topic.name }}"></option>
              {% endfor %}
            </datalist>
            <details class="filter-help" data-preserve-attr="open">
              <summary>Filter syntax</summary>
              <ul>
                <li><code>1D-1M-1N</code> a bid; suits C D H S N. Separate calls with a dash or a
                  space — <code>1D 1H</code>, <code>1D-1H</code> and <code>1D--1H</code> are the same</li>
                <li><code>M</code> / <code>m</code> any major / any minor — <em>the only place case matters</em></li>
                <li><code>1*</code> / <code>*</code> any suit at that level / any call</li>
                <li><code>Pass</code> <code>X</code> <code>XX</code> pass, double, redouble</li>
                <li><code>(2H)</code> brackets = the opponents' call; <code>(*)</code> = they did something</li>
                <li><code>1D-1M, 2C</code> comma separates alternatives — either one matches</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                A pattern describes <strong>your</strong> auction: opponent calls you do not mention
                are stepped over, so <code>1D-1H</code> matches 1D (Pass) 1H, 1D (1S) 1H and
                1D (X) 1H. Bracket a call to pin the opponents down at that exact point. Too few
                matches falls back to the whole system.
              </p>
            </details>
          </details>

          <p class="hint"><em>Changes restart the quiz.</em></p>
          {# Restart also CLOSES the drawer, but only where the drawer is an overlay. Below 900px it
             covers the quiz, so "start again" that leaves you looking at the settings panel you just
             used is a second tap for no reason; above it the drawer is a column beside the quiz and
             closing it would only take the controls away. The same test the Topics button applies,
             for the same reason, off the same constant.
             Only the explicit button does this -- the sliders and checkboxes restart the quiz too,
             and you may be adjusting several. #}
          <button type="button" class="danger button is-danger"
                  data-on:click="@post('{{ prefix }}/restart{{ variant_query }}');
                    window.matchMedia('{{ DRAWER_OVERLAY_QUERY }}').matches && ($_navOpen = false)">Restart</button>
          {# Presentation, not quiz rules -- and collapsed, because the settings column was the
             tallest thing on the page. With these folded away it fits an ordinary viewport without a
             scroll region, which is what stops the last panel looking clipped.
             `data-bind` in the *value* form: both signal names start with an underscore. #}
          <details class="appearance" data-preserve-attr="open">
            <summary>Appearance</summary>
            {# Both pickers hand focus BACK after they commit. A `<select>` keeps focus once clicked,
               and the digit accelerators ignore keystrokes aimed at a form control -- so picking a
               font or a stylesheet silently disabled 1-9 for the rest of the session, until the
               player happened to click somewhere else. These two are safe to blur because a change
               is the whole interaction; the difficulty slider and the filter box are NOT (you keep
               arrowing / typing in them), which is why the blur is here and not on `.controls`. #}
            {# The Bulma option is the reason for the `class="light button is-light"` pairs
               throughout these templates: Bulma is class-based, so the framework's class strings
               have to be IN the markup, and they ship on every patch whichever stylesheet is
               active. Harmless when it is not -- `.button` / `.box` / `.notification` / `.input`
               / `.tag` are names neither `app.css` nor `app-pico.css` uses -- but that is the
               cost the comparison is measuring, so it is paid in the open rather than gated
               behind a `data-class` per element. #}
            {# DEBUG ONLY. The A/B/C is settled -- everyone gets `render.DEFAULT_CSS` -- and the
               three variants differ in details a player has no way to care about, so offering the
               choice was asking them to make a decision with nothing on either side of it. The
               picker stays because the comparison is still the point of the spike (COMPARISON.md)
               and switching sheets live is how it is checked; it is just behind `?debug` now, with
               the rest of the machinery that exists for us rather than for them. #}
            {% if debug %}
            <label>
              Base CSS
              {# Bulma wants `<div class="select">` AROUND the select -- a wrapper element, not a
                 class on the select. A plain div for the other two sheets, which is exactly the
                 kind of markup tax that does not show up in a line count. #}
              <div class="select">
                <select data-bind="_css" data-on:change="evt.target.blur()">
                  <option value="hand">Hand-rolled</option>
                  <option value="pico">Pico classless</option>
                  <option value="bulma">Bulma (spike)</option>
                </select>
              </div>
            </label>
            {% endif %}
            {# The game-feel experiment, on by default. `checked` is rendered statically to match the
               declared default of `$_juice`: an unchecked box would be what `data-bind` uploads into
               the signal on first paint, turning it off before anyone chose to. #}
            <label class="checkbox">
              <input type="checkbox" checked data-bind="_juice" />
              Game feel (shake, floating points, streak)
            </label>
            {# Sound is the one appearance preference that starts OFF, and NOT for the usual
               conservative reason: the others change how the page looks to the person who asked for
               them, while audio arrives in whatever room the laptop is in. Note the missing `checked`
               -- it has to agree with the declared default of `$_sound` (see `render.local_ui_signals`),
               or the first paint would upload `true` into the signal and the quiz would start making
               noise on its own. Turning it on is also what gives the five `<audio>` elements their
               `src`, so this box is the fetch as well as the switch. #}
            <label class="checkbox">
              <input type="checkbox" data-bind="_sound" />
              Sound (countdown tick, scoring chimes)
            </label>
            <label>
              Font
              <div class="select">
                <select data-bind="_font" data-on:change="evt.target.blur()">
                <option value="notes">Open Sans (as the notes)</option>
                <option value="system">System UI</option>
                <option value="rounded">Rounded</option>
                <option value="serif">Serif</option>
                <option value="mono">Monospace</option>
                </select>
              </div>
            </label>
          </details>
        </section>
      </aside>

      <main class="main">
        {# the one fragment that is patched as elements #}
        {# `card` is OUR class and also one of Bulma's -- and for once they mean the same thing, so
           the Bulma variant inherits its surface for free and the adapter only adds the padding
           (Bulma's card expects a `.card-content` child) and the green top rule. #}
        {# the one fragment that is patched as elements #}
        {# `card` is OUR class and also one of Bulma's -- and for once they mean the same thing, so
           the Bulma variant inherits its surface for free and the adapter only adds the padding
           (Bulma's card expects a `.card-content` child) and the green top rule. #}
        {# COMING BACK FROM THE SYSTEM NOTES TAKES THE KEYBOARD BACK.

           The reported bug, reproduced: read the notes, click inside them to scroll, come back to
           the quiz, and 1-9 do nothing while the mouse still works. Nothing in this app is broken
           when that happens -- the notes are an `<iframe>`, so a click inside them puts focus in
           ANOTHER DOCUMENT (a cross-origin one), and every accelerator here is a `__window` listener
           on ours. The keys are being delivered, just not to us, and no guard we own can see it:
           `$_answering`, `$_topicsOpen` and the qid are all fine, which is exactly why it looked
           like "the shortcuts randomly stop working". The mouse keeps working because a click is
           delivered by position, not by focus.

           Bringing the pointer back to the question is the gesture that means "I am playing again",
           so that is where the focus is taken back. Narrow on purpose -- only when the thing holding
           focus is an iframe, so a half-typed filter box or a focused slider is left alone -- and it
           costs nothing when focus is already ours. `window.focus()` as well as the blur, because
           the parent document has to be the one holding focus, not merely the iframe not holding it. #}
        <section class="card" id="quiz"
                 data-on:mouseenter="document.activeElement?.tagName === 'IFRAME'
                   && (document.activeElement.blur(), window.focus())">{{ quiz_body | safe }}</section>

        {# The timer's markup never changes; only the signal does. Which side produces that signal
           is the experiment (DSQUIZ_TIMER, see app.TIMER_MODE):

           client -- the browser walks $_timeLeftPct down from the allowance the server stated with
                     the question. No connection held, no per-tick server work.
           stream -- a held SSE connection pushes the value, which is panel's model exactly.

           Either way nothing here is trusted for scoring: the bonus is recomputed server-side from
           the question's start time when the answer arrives. #}
        {# In stream mode the connection is opened by `data-init` on <body>, NOT here: this element is
           inside the morph target, so a per-patch `data-init` would open a fresh held connection on
           every interaction. The client-interval expression is safe to re-create -- it is idempotent
           and the signal it drives lives in the store, not the DOM.

           The interval used to gate on `$_playing`, which is only false once the whole quiz is over
           -- so the bar kept draining after a question was answered: down to empty behind the reveal,
           and through the two or three seconds of toasts on a correct answer. `$_ticking` is the
           server's "a live, unanswered question is being timed", and `$_answering` covers the window
           between the click and the patch that ends it. Assigning ONLY while ticking is what freezes
           the bar rather than resetting it: the value it holds is what was left when the answer
           landed, which is the number the bonus was scored from. #}
        {# The last three seconds also TICK, when sound is on. It rides this interval rather than
           having one of its own, which raises the obvious problem: the interval runs at 100ms and a
           tick per run is a 10Hz buzz. The rate limit is the SAMPLE -- `sfx.py` pads the blip with
           silence out to a full second, and `play()` on an element that is already playing does
           nothing, so the sound's own length spaces the ticks and no timer state is kept anywhere.

           `$_timeLeftPct * $_questionMs < 300000` is "under three seconds left" without a division
           (pct x ms < 3000 x 100). The assignment before it returns the new percentage, so at zero
           the chain stops on its own -- there is no tick on an expired clock.

           Client-timer only. `DSQUIZ_TIMER=stream` omits this attribute entirely (the server pushes
           the percentage instead), and a tick would then have to be an element patch per second per
           tab, which is a cost the comparison should not quietly absorb. #}
        <div class="timer" id="timer"
             {% if timer_mode != "stream" %}data-on-interval__duration.100ms="$_ticking && !$_answering && ($_timeLeftPct = Math.max(0, $_timeLeftPct - 10000 / $_questionMs)) && $_sound && $_timeLeftPct * $_questionMs < 300000 && document.getElementById('sfx-tick')?.play()?.catch(() => {})"{% endif %}>
          {# `ticking` is the same condition the interval above assigns under, and it is here for the
             game-feel layer: the last band throbs, and a bar that is FROZEN behind a reveal (or mid
             answer-stream) must not -- urgency about a question you have already answered is a lie.
             The bands themselves stay purely a function of the percentage, as they were. #}
          <div class="timer-fill" data-style:width="$_timeLeftPct + '%'"
               data-class="{spent: $_timeLeftPct < 17, low: $_timeLeftPct >= 17 && $_timeLeftPct < 49, mid: $_timeLeftPct >= 49 && $_timeLeftPct < 65, high: $_timeLeftPct >= 65, ticking: $_ticking && !$_answering}"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="toasts" id="toasts" aria-live="polite"></div>

        {% if topics %}{% include "topics.html.j2" %}{% endif %}

        <details class="notes" data-preserve-attr="open">
          <summary>Notation</summary>
          <ul>
            <li>Bids in brackets e.g (1♥), (bid), (any), (1NT) etc. indicate the opponents made the bid.</li>
            <li>The opponents' bids are often automatically removed from the question</li>
            <li>~ means roughly/approximately (points are guides, not absolute)</li>
            <li>X or Dbl means double</li>
            <li>GF/FG means game forcing</li>
            <li>NF means non-forcing</li>
            <li>M means major, oM other major</li>
            <li>m means minor, om other minor</li>
            <li>Hx/HHx means Honour + x (small card), Honour Honour x etc.</li>
          </ul>
        </details>

        <details class="notes" data-preserve-attr="open">
          <summary>System Notes</summary>
          <iframe src="{{ variant.system_notes_url }}" title="system notes" loading="lazy"></iframe>
        </details>
      </main>
    </div>
